2. Deep Latent Gaussian Models
Deep latent Gaussian models (DLGMs) are a general class of deep directed graphical models that consist of Gaussian latent variables at each layer of a processing hierarchy. The model consists of L layers of latent variables. To generate a sample from the model, we begin at the top-most layer (L) by drawing from a Gaussian distribution. The activation hl at any lower layer is formed by a non-linear transformation of the layer above hl+1, perturbed by Gaussian noise. We descend through the hierarchy and generate observations v by sampling from the observation likelihood using the activation of the lowest layer h1. This process is described graphically in figure 1(a).
